/AggregateMapper.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/AggregateMapper.java","srcChanges":[{"pos":9725,"endPos":10205,"startLine":202,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":212,"endLineOffset":5,"length":481}],"dstChanges":[{"pos":6809,"endPos":7305,"startLine":144,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":153,"endLineOffset":5,"length":497}]},{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/AggregateMapper.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/AggregateMapper.java-6151:6198,6207:6346,6495:6598,6644:6766-6189:6204,6256:6400,6549:6650,6699:6797","promptId":"#K77JN","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/AggregateMapper.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/AggregateMapper.java","srcChanges":[{"pos":6151,"endPos":6198,"startLine":133,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":133,"endLineOffset":55,"length":48},{"pos":6207,"endPos":6346,"startLine":134,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":138,"endLineOffset":9,"length":140},{"pos":6495,"endPos":6598,"startLine":140,"startLineOffset":12,"endLine":140,"endLineOffset":115,"length":104},{"pos":6644,"endPos":6766,"startLine":142,"startLineOffset":15,"endLine":144,"endLineOffset":9,"length":123}],"dstChanges":[{"pos":6189,"endPos":6204,"startLine":133,"startLineOffset":60,"endLine":133,"endLineOffset":75,"length":16},{"pos":6256,"endPos":6400,"startLine":134,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":136,"endLineOffset":9,"length":145},{"pos":6549,"endPos":6650,"startLine":138,"startLineOffset":12,"endLine":138,"endLineOffset":113,"length":102},{"pos":6699,"endPos":6797,"startLine":141,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":141,"endLineOffset":106,"length":99}]}]}],"groundTruthGeneratedCommentsNodes":[{"groundTruth":{"original_line":98,"start_side":"RIGHT","start_line":96,"line":98,"comment_lines":["[][96]","[][97]","[][98]"],"review_comment":["Oh, this is very interesting. Thanks for catching this.\r\n\r\nI\u0027m wondering if enforcing unique names for `PhysicalPlan` outputs is actually the right way to go. It is what we assumed so far, so I don\u0027t wanna depart from here, but there\u0027s also a reason to be more lenient:\r\n\r\nIn PhysicalPlan land, this output method was _technically_ correct, because it represented exactly what the physical operators will do: they\u0027re gonna append the blocks for the added fields to the pages that are processed. The blocks corresponding to shadowed attributes/channels will _not_ be stripped from incoming pages.\r\n\r\nThis means that the output layout technically has duplicate attribute names. This change just hides them and makes them inaccessible during the physical planning stage.\r\n\r\nFWIW, `EvalExec` has the exact same situation. `Eval` performs shadowing, but `EvalOperator` can only ever append blocks, so the output pages actually still contain the blocks corresponding to shadowed attributes."],"path":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/physical/LookupJoinExec.java","id":36728,"created_at":"2024-12-10T10:51:47Z","original_position":9,"side":"RIGHT","position":9,"original_start_line":96,"submitted_on_commit":"d3b3dc627eaf45d3a255b1e3e5293fc31b395231","diff_hunk":"@@ -93,9 +93,9 @@ public List\u003cAttribute\u003e addedFields() {\n     public List\u003cAttribute\u003e output() {\n         if (lazyOutput \u003d\u003d null) {\n             lazyOutput \u003d new ArrayList\u003c\u003e(left().output());\n-            for (Attribute attr : addedFields) {\n-                lazyOutput.add(attr);\n-            }\n+            var addedFieldsNames \u003d addedFields.stream().map(Attribute::name).toList();\n+            lazyOutput.removeIf(a -\u003e addedFieldsNames.contains(a.name()));\n+            lazyOutput.addAll(addedFields);","submitted_start_line":96,"submitted_line":98},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]}],"coveredGroundTruth":0,"uncoveredGroundTruth":1,"coveredMatchable":20,"uncoveredMatchable":21,"timing":55227,"tokensIn":10221,"tokensOut":1443}